  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Rafael Devers kept them alive. Twice. But Dylan Smith let it die in the eighth.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed early, clawed back twice, and held a lead into the seventh. The offense got to ten hits and six runs on a day that felt like it should have been enough. Drew Gilbert went 4-for-4 with a home run and three RBIs, and the lineup strung together two-run innings in the second and fifth to turn the game into a fight. But fights need finishes. In the eighth, Smith gave up three runs in one inning. One swing, three outs, and a 6-7 loss in Denver.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what inconsistency looks like when it hardens into a season: a team that can generate enough offense to lead through six innings but cannot close. Eight runners left on base tells you the offense was there. It also tells you the one run they converted in eight RISP at-bats was not nearly enough. The lead lasted two innings. That's not execution. That's fragility wearing the same uniform every day.",
